// Class 11 Physics practice bank, part 1: chapters 1-7 (Units and Measurement to Gravitation).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "physics", {
  "units-and-measurement": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of luminous intensity is:", options: ["lumen", "candela", "lux", "watt"], answer: 1, explain: "Luminous intensity is one of the seven base quantities; its SI base unit is the candela (cd).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a dimensionless quantity?", options: ["Strain", "Stress", "Pressure", "Torque"], answer: 0, explain: "Strain = change in length / original length, a ratio of like quantities. Stress and pressure are [M L⁻¹ T⁻²] and torque is [M L² T⁻²].", level: 1 },
      { q: "The dimensional formula of impulse is:", options: ["[M L T⁻²]", "[M L² T⁻¹]", "[M L T⁻¹]", "[M L² T⁻²]"], answer: 2, explain: "Impulse = force × time = [M L T⁻²][T] = [M L T⁻¹], the same as momentum.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The radian is the SI unit of:", options: ["solid angle", "frequency", "angular momentum", "plane angle"], answer: 3, explain: "Plane angle θ = arc/radius is measured in radian; solid angle is measured in steradian.", level: 1 },
      { q: "One parsec is approximately equal to:", options: ["3.08 × 10¹⁶ m", "9.46 × 10¹⁵ m", "1.496 × 10¹¹ m", "3 × 10⁸ m"], answer: 0, explain: "1 parsec ≈ 3.08 × 10¹⁶ m. The other values are one light year and one astronomical unit, and the speed of light in m s⁻¹.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A cyclist and her bicycle have a total mass of 80 kg and move at 54 km/h. Her kinetic energy, ½mv², in joule is:", options: ["116640 J", "1200 J", "18000 J", "9000 J"], answer: 3, explain: "54 km/h = 54 × 1000/3600 = 15 m/s. K = ½ × 80 × 15² = 40 × 225 = 9000 J. Using 54 directly (116640 J) is the unit-conversion mistake.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The equation T = 2π√(l/g) for a simple pendulum cannot be derived completely by dimensional analysis.\nReason (R): Dimensional analysis cannot determine dimensionless constants such as 2π.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Dimensions give T ∝ √(l/g) but the numerical factor 2π has no dimensions and so cannot be found. R is the correct reason for A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "From E = hν, the dimensional formula of Planck's constant h is:", options: ["[M L² T⁻²]", "[M L² T⁻¹]", "[M L T⁻¹]", "[M L² T⁻³]"], answer: 1, explain: "h = E/ν = [M L² T⁻²]/[T⁻¹] = [M L² T⁻¹], the same as angular momentum.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A carpenter measures a tabletop as 3.25 m long and 1.2 m wide. The area, to the correct number of significant figures, is:", options: ["3.90 m²", "4 m²", "3.9 m²", "3.900 m²"], answer: 2, explain: "3.25 × 1.2 = 3.900. The breadth has only 2 significant figures, so the area is quoted to 2: 3.9 m².", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Pressure and Young's modulus have the same dimensions.\nReason (R): Quantities with the same dimensions must be the same physical quantity.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Both are [M L⁻¹ T⁻²], so A is true. R is false: work and torque, for example, have the same dimensions but are different quantities.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },

      { q: "The frequency f of a stretched string depends on its length l, the tension F in it and its mass per unit length μ. Dimensional analysis gives f ∝ lᵃ Fᵇ μᶜ with (a, b, c) equal to:", options: ["(1, ½, −½)", "(−1, −½, ½)", "(−½, ½, −1)", "(−1, ½, −½)"], answer: 3, explain: "[T⁻¹] = Lᵃ (M L T⁻²)ᵇ (M L⁻¹)ᶜ. M: b + c = 0. T: −2b = −1 so b = ½, c = −½. L: a + b − c = 0 so a = −1. Hence f ∝ (1/l)√(F/μ).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A new system of units uses 2 kg as the unit of mass, 5 m as the unit of length and 10 s as the unit of time. A force of 20 N in this system is:", options: ["2 units", "20 units", "200 units", "2000 units"], answer: 2, explain: "Unit of force = 2 × 5 / 10² = 0.1 N. So 20 N = 20 / 0.1 = 200 new units.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The time period T of oscillation of a liquid drop depends on its density ρ, radius r and surface tension S (force per unit length). Which relation is dimensionally correct?", options: ["T ∝ ρ^½ r^½ S^(−½)", "T ∝ ρ^½ r^(3/2) S^(−½)", "T ∝ ρ^(−½) r^(3/2) S^½", "T ∝ ρ r³ S⁻¹"], answer: 1, explain: "[S] = [M T⁻²]. T = ρᵃ rᵇ Sᶜ gives M: a + c = 0; L: −3a + b = 0; T: −2c = 1. So c = −½, a = ½, b = 3/2: T ∝ √(ρ r³/S).", level: 3 },
      { q: "In the relation P = (α/β) exp(−αz / kθ), P is pressure, z is distance, k is Boltzmann constant and θ is temperature. The dimensional formula of β is:", options: ["[M⁰ L² T⁰]", "[M L² T⁰]", "[M⁰ L T⁰]", "[M L T⁻²]"], answer: 0, explain: "kθ is an energy, [M L² T⁻²], and αz/kθ is dimensionless, so [α] = [M L² T⁻²]/[L] = [M L T⁻²]. Then [β] = [α]/[P] = [M L T⁻²]/[M L⁻¹ T⁻²] = [L²].", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the dimensional formula of angular momentum.", a: "L = r × p = [L][M L T⁻¹] = **[M L² T⁻¹]**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the number of significant figures in 0.003040 and in 5.00 × 10³.", a: "0.003040 has **4** (3, 0, 4, 0: leading zeros are not significant, the trailing zero after the decimal point is). 5.00 × 10³ has **3**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Check by the method of dimensions whether the relation P = ρgh is correct, where P is pressure, ρ density and h height.", a: "[P] = [M L⁻¹ T⁻²].\n[ρgh] = [M L⁻³][L T⁻²][L] = [M L⁻¹ T⁻²].\nBoth sides have the same dimensions, so the relation is **dimensionally correct**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Round off 6.835 and 6.845 to three significant figures using the rule for a dropped digit 5.", a: "If the dropped digit is exactly 5, the preceding digit is raised by 1 only if it is odd; otherwise it is left unchanged.\n6.835: preceding digit 3 is odd, so it becomes **6.84**.\n6.845: preceding digit 4 is even, so it stays **6.84**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Using dimensional analysis, derive an expression for the centripetal force F on a body of mass m moving with speed v in a circle of radius r.", a: "Let F = k mᵃ vᵇ rᶜ, with k dimensionless.\n[M L T⁻²] = [M]ᵃ [L T⁻¹]ᵇ [L]ᶜ = [Mᵃ Lᵇ⁺ᶜ T⁻ᵇ].\nM: a = 1. T: b = 2. L: b + c = 1, so c = −1.\nHence **F = k mv²/r**; the value k = 1 cannot be found by this method (experiment gives k = 1).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Convert g = 9.8 m s⁻² into km h⁻² using the dimensional method.", a: "[g] = [L T⁻²], so n₂ = n₁ (L₁/L₂)(T₁/T₂)⁻².\nL₁/L₂ = 1 m / 1 km = 10⁻³; T₁/T₂ = 1 s / 1 h = 1/3600, so (T₁/T₂)⁻² = 3600² = 1.296 × 10⁷.\nn₂ = 9.8 × 10⁻³ × 1.296 × 10⁷ = **1.27 × 10⁵** (127008).\nSo g ≈ 1.27 × 10⁵ km h⁻².", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The speed v of a wave in a gas depends on the pressure P and the density ρ of the gas as v = k Pᵃ ρᵇ. Find a and b by dimensional analysis and write the relation.", a: "[L T⁻¹] = [M L⁻¹ T⁻²]ᵃ [M L⁻³]ᵇ.\nM: a + b = 0. T: −2a = −1, so a = ½ and b = −½.\nCheck L: −a − 3b = −½ + 3/2 = 1 ✓.\nHence **v = k √(P/ρ)**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "(a) Check the dimensional correctness of (i) s = ut + ½at², (ii) E = mc², (iii) v = u + at². (b) A student says that a dimensionally correct equation must be physically correct. Is she right? Give a reason.", a: "(a) (i) [s] = [L]; [ut] = [L T⁻¹][T] = [L]; [½at²] = [L T⁻²][T²] = [L]. All terms agree: **correct**.\n(ii) [E] = [M L² T⁻²]; [mc²] = [M][L T⁻¹]² = [M L² T⁻²]: **correct**.\n(iii) [v] = [L T⁻¹] but [at²] = [L T⁻²][T²] = [L]: **dimensionally wrong**; it should be v = u + at.\n(b) She is not right. Dimensional agreement is necessary but not sufficient: the equation can still be wrong in numerical factors (for instance s = ut + at² is dimensionally fine but physically wrong), because dimensional analysis cannot detect dimensionless constants.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "State the rules for finding the number of significant figures in the result of (i) multiplication or division and (ii) addition or subtraction. Apply them to: (a) 12.9 + 1.03 + 0.056, (b) 4.2 × 3.14, (c) 7.40 ÷ 2.5.", a: "(i) For × or ÷, the result keeps as many significant figures as the quantity with the **fewest significant figures**.\n(ii) For + or −, the result keeps as many **decimal places** as the quantity with the fewest decimal places.\n(a) 12.9 + 1.03 + 0.056 = 13.986; fewest decimals is 1 (12.9), so **14.0**.\n(b) 4.2 × 3.14 = 13.188; 4.2 has 2 significant figures, so **13**.\n(c) 7.40 ÷ 2.5 = 2.96; 2.5 has 2 significant figures, so **3.0**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A student proposes that the speed of sound in a fluid is v = √(B/ρ), where B is the bulk modulus (it has the same dimensions as pressure) and ρ is the density.\n(i) Write the dimensions of B.\n(ii) Show that the proposed relation is dimensionally consistent.\n(iii) Another student suggests v = 2√(B/ρ). Does dimensional analysis rule it out? What does this show?", a: "(i) [B] = [pressure] = **[M L⁻¹ T⁻²]**.\n(ii) [B/ρ] = [M L⁻¹ T⁻²]/[M L⁻³] = [L² T⁻²], so [√(B/ρ)] = [L T⁻¹] = [v] ✓.\n(iii) **No.** The factor 2 is dimensionless, so v = 2√(B/ρ) is also dimensionally consistent. This shows that dimensional analysis cannot determine dimensionless numerical constants; experiment or theory is needed.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "motion-in-a-straight-line": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The slope of the position–time graph of a particle gives its:", options: ["acceleration", "displacement", "velocity", "distance"], answer: 2, explain: "Slope = dx/dt, which is the (instantaneous) velocity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The area under the velocity–time graph of a particle between two instants gives its:", options: ["acceleration", "displacement", "jerk", "average speed"], answer: 1, explain: "Displacement = ∫v dt, the signed area under the v–t graph.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A stone is thrown vertically upward. At the highest point of its path:", options: ["v = 0 and a = 0", "v = g and a = 0", "v is maximum and a = g", "v = 0 and a = g, directed downward"], answer: 3, explain: "The velocity reverses there, so it is momentarily zero, but gravity still acts: a = g downward.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A particle completes one full revolution of a circular track of radius r. Its displacement and the distance covered are respectively:", options: ["0 and 2πr", "2πr and 0", "0 and 0", "2r and 2πr"], answer: 0, explain: "It returns to the starting point, so displacement is zero; the distance is the circumference 2πr.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the absence of air, a heavy stone and a feather released together from the same height:", options: ["the stone reaches the ground first", "the feather reaches the ground first", "reach the ground at the same time", "reach at times that depend on their masses"], answer: 2, explain: "In vacuum both have the same acceleration g, so they land together.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A car moving at 72 km/h applies its brakes and slows uniformly at 5 m/s². The distance it travels before stopping is:", options: ["40 m", "80 m", "20 m", "4 m"], answer: 0, explain: "u = 72 km/h = 20 m/s. From v² = u² − 2as: s = 400/(2 × 5) = 40 m.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A body can have zero velocity and non-zero acceleration at the same instant.\nReason (R): Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, not the velocity itself.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "At the top of a vertical throw v = 0 while a = g. This is possible because a = dv/dt depends on how v is changing, not on its value.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A ball is dropped from a bridge 80 m above a river (take g = 10 m/s²). It reaches the water after:", options: ["8 s", "4 s", "2 s", "16 s"], answer: 1, explain: "h = ½gt² gives t = √(2h/g) = √(160/10) = √16 = 4 s.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student cycles to school covering the first half of the distance at 20 km/h and the second half at 30 km/h. Her average speed for the whole trip is:", options: ["25 km/h", "26 km/h", "24 km/h", "50 km/h"], answer: 2, explain: "For equal distances, average speed = 2v₁v₂/(v₁ + v₂) = 2 × 20 × 30/50 = 24 km/h, not the simple mean 25 km/h.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A body moving with a negative acceleration must be slowing down.\nReason (R): The sign of acceleration only shows its direction relative to the chosen positive axis.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "If a body moves in the negative direction and its acceleration is also negative, its speed increases. So A is false; R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },

      { q: "A ball is thrown vertically upward with a speed of 20 m/s (g = 10 m/s²). The distance travelled by it in the first 3 s is:", options: ["15 m", "25 m", "30 m", "45 m"], answer: 1, explain: "It rises for 2 s to a height of 20 m, then falls for 1 s through ½ × 10 × 1² = 5 m. Distance = 25 m (the displacement is 15 m).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A stone dropped from the top of a tower covers 45 m in the last second of its fall (g = 10 m/s²). The height of the tower is:", options: ["45 m", "100 m", "245 m", "125 m"], answer: 3, explain: "Distance in the nth second = ½g(2n − 1) = 5(2n − 1) = 45, so n = 5. Height = ½ × 10 × 5² = 125 m (check: 125 − 80 = 45 m).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two trains of lengths 150 m and 100 m run on parallel tracks in opposite directions at 54 km/h and 36 km/h. The time taken to cross each other completely is:", options: ["25 s", "6 s", "10 s", "5 s"], answer: 2, explain: "Relative speed = 54 + 36 = 90 km/h = 25 m/s. Distance to be covered = 150 + 100 = 250 m, so t = 250/25 = 10 s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The position of a particle is x = t³ − 3t² (x in m, t in s). The distance travelled in the first 3 s is:", options: ["8 m", "0", "4 m", "12 m"], answer: 0, explain: "v = 3t² − 6t = 0 at t = 2 s. x(0) = 0, x(2) = −4 m, x(3) = 0. Distance = 4 + 4 = 8 m, although the displacement is 0.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Can a body have zero average velocity but non-zero average speed? Give an example.", a: "Yes. If a body returns to its starting point, the displacement and so the average velocity are zero, but the distance travelled (and hence the average speed) is not. Example: a runner completing one lap of a track.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What do the slope and the area of a velocity–time graph represent?", a: "The **slope** of a v–t graph gives the acceleration; the **area** under it gives the displacement.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 3 m/s² for 8 s. Find its final velocity and the distance covered.", a: "v = u + at = 0 + 3 × 8 = **24 m/s**.\ns = ut + ½at² = ½ × 3 × 8² = **96 m**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A ball is thrown vertically upward with a speed of 19.6 m/s (g = 9.8 m/s²). Find the time to reach the highest point and the maximum height.", a: "At the top v = 0: t = u/g = 19.6/9.8 = **2 s**.\nH = u²/2g = (19.6)²/(2 × 9.8) = 384.16/19.6 = **19.6 m**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Using a velocity–time graph, derive the relation s = ut + ½at² for uniformly accelerated motion.", a: "1. Draw the v–t graph: a straight line from v = u at t = 0 to v = v at time t.\n2. Displacement s = area under the graph = rectangle (u × t) + triangle (½ × t × (v − u)).\n3. Since v − u = at, the triangle area is ½ × t × at = ½at².\nHence **s = ut + ½at²**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A train moving at 90 km/h is slowed uniformly to 54 km/h in 10 s. Find (i) the deceleration, (ii) the distance covered in these 10 s, (iii) the further distance it travels before stopping if the deceleration remains the same.", a: "u = 90 km/h = 25 m/s, v = 54 km/h = 15 m/s.\n(i) a = (15 − 25)/10 = −1, so the deceleration is **1 m/s²**.\n(ii) s = (u + v)t/2 = (25 + 15) × 10/2 = **200 m**.\n(iii) From 15 m/s to rest: s = v²/2a = 225/2 = **112.5 m**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Car A is 100 m behind car B on a straight road. A moves at 25 m/s and B at 15 m/s in the same direction. Find the time taken by A to overtake B and the distance A travels in that time.", a: "Relative velocity of A with respect to B = 25 − 15 = 10 m/s.\nTime to close the 100 m gap: t = 100/10 = **10 s**.\nDistance travelled by A = 25 × 10 = **250 m** (B travels 150 m; 250 − 150 = 100 m ✓).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the three equations of uniformly accelerated motion, v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as, using a velocity–time graph.", a: "Let the velocity change uniformly from u to v in time t; the graph is a straight line.\n1. Slope = acceleration: a = (v − u)/t, so **v = u + at**.\n2. Displacement = area under the graph = ut + ½(v − u)t = ut + ½at², so **s = ut + ½at²**.\n3. Area of the trapezium: s = ½(u + v)t. Substitute t = (v − u)/a: s = (v + u)(v − u)/2a = (v² − u²)/2a, so **v² = u² + 2as**.\nThese hold only for constant acceleration.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A stone is dropped from the top of a 45 m tower. At the same instant another stone is thrown vertically upward from the ground with a speed of 30 m/s (g = 10 m/s²). Find when and where they meet, and their speeds then.", a: "Take the relative motion: both have the same acceleration g, so the relative acceleration is zero. Relative speed = 30 m/s (constant), initial gap 45 m.\nTime to meet: t = 45/30 = **1.5 s**.\nStone 1 has fallen ½ × 10 × 1.5² = 11.25 m, so they meet **33.75 m above the ground**. (Check with stone 2: 30 × 1.5 − 11.25 = 33.75 m ✓.)\nSpeeds: stone 1: 10 × 1.5 = 15 m/s downward; stone 2: 30 − 15 = 15 m/s upward.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A metro train starts from rest at one station and accelerates uniformly at 1.5 m/s² for 20 s. It then moves at constant speed for 60 s, and finally brakes uniformly to rest in the next 10 s.\n(i) Find the maximum speed of the train.\n(ii) Find the total distance between the two stations.\n(iii) Find the average speed for the whole journey.", a: "(i) v = at = 1.5 × 20 = **30 m/s**.\n(ii) Acceleration phase: ½ × 30 × 20 = 300 m. Constant speed: 30 × 60 = 1800 m. Braking: ½ × 30 × 10 = 150 m.\nTotal = 300 + 1800 + 150 = **2250 m**.\n(iii) Total time = 20 + 60 + 10 = 90 s, so average speed = 2250/90 = **25 m/s**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "motion-in-a-plane": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following is a scalar quantity?", options: ["velocity", "displacement", "speed", "acceleration"], answer: 2, explain: "Speed has magnitude only. Velocity, displacement and acceleration all need a direction.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For a given speed of projection on level ground (no air resistance), the horizontal range is maximum when the angle of projection is:", options: ["30°", "45°", "60°", "90°"], answer: 1, explain: "R = u² sin2θ/g is maximum when sin2θ = 1, i.e. θ = 45°.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In uniform circular motion, which of the following remains constant?", options: ["speed", "velocity", "acceleration", "momentum"], answer: 0, explain: "The speed is constant, but the direction of velocity (hence velocity, momentum and the acceleration vector) keeps changing.", level: 1 },
      { q: "At the highest point of its path, the velocity of a projectile (projected at an angle less than 90°) is:", options: ["zero", "vertical and downward", "directed along 45°", "horizontal"], answer: 3, explain: "The vertical component becomes zero there; only the constant horizontal component u cosθ remains.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The magnitude of the cross product A × B is maximum when the angle between A and B is:", options: ["0°", "45°", "90°", "180°"], answer: 2, explain: "|A × B| = AB sinθ, which is largest when sinθ = 1, i.e. θ = 90°.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A footballer kicks a ball at 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal (g = 10 m/s²). The maximum height reached by the ball is:", options: ["10 m", "5 m", "20 m", "2.5 m"], answer: 1, explain: "The vertical component is 20 sin30° = 10 m/s. H = (10)²/(2 × 10) = 5 m.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A boat is steered straight across a river 120 m wide at 4 m/s relative to the water. The river flows at 3 m/s. The boat lands downstream of the starting point by:", options: ["120 m", "160 m", "40 m", "90 m"], answer: 3, explain: "Crossing time = 120/4 = 30 s. In this time the current carries it 3 × 30 = 90 m downstream.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The horizontal component of the velocity of a projectile remains constant during its flight (air resistance neglected).\nReason (R): No force acts on the projectile in the horizontal direction.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Gravity acts vertically, so the horizontal acceleration is zero and u cosθ stays constant.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A projectile fired at 15° to the horizontal has a range R. With the same speed, the range will again be R if it is fired at:", options: ["75°", "30°", "60°", "45°"], answer: 0, explain: "R ∝ sin2θ, and sin2θ is the same for θ and 90° − θ. So 15° and 75° give equal ranges.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The angular velocity of a particle in uniform circular motion is constant.\nReason (R): The linear velocity of a particle in uniform circular motion is constant.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "ω = v/r stays fixed because the speed and radius are fixed, so A is true. The linear velocity changes direction continuously, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },

      { q: "A projectile is fired with a speed of 40 m/s at an angle of 37° above the horizontal (sin37° = 0.6, cos37° = 0.8, g = 10 m/s²). Its horizontal range is:", options: ["76.8 m", "115.2 m", "153.6 m", "192 m"], answer: 2, explain: "uₓ = 32 m/s and u_y = 24 m/s. T = 2 × 24/10 = 4.8 s, so R = 32 × 4.8 = 153.6 m.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The horizontal range of a projectile is four times its maximum height. The angle of projection is:", options: ["30°", "45°", "60°", "tan⁻¹ 2"], answer: 1, explain: "H = u² sin²θ/2g and R = 2u² sinθ cosθ/g, so R/H = 4 cotθ. R = 4H gives cotθ = 1, θ = 45°.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A stone is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from the top of an 80 m high cliff (g = 10 m/s²). The speed with which it hits the water below is nearly:", options: ["55 m/s", "40 m/s", "25 m/s", "42.7 m/s"], answer: 3, explain: "Fall time = √(2 × 80/10) = 4 s, so v_y = 40 m/s. Speed = √(15² + 40²) = √1825 ≈ 42.7 m/s. Simply adding the components gives the wrong 55 m/s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A cyclist rides west at 8 m/s in vertically falling rain whose speed is 6 m/s. To keep dry, she should hold her umbrella:", options: ["at tan⁻¹(4/3) to the vertical, tilted towards the west", "at tan⁻¹(4/3) to the vertical, tilted towards the east", "at tan⁻¹(3/4) to the vertical, tilted towards the west", "at tan⁻¹(3/4) to the vertical, tilted towards the east"], answer: 0, explain: "Velocity of rain relative to the cyclist = v_rain − v_cyclist = (0, −6) − (−8, 0) = (+8, −6): 10 m/s coming from the front (west). The angle with the vertical satisfies tanφ = 8/6 = 4/3, so the umbrella is tilted forward (west).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the expression for the centripetal acceleration of a particle moving in a circle of radius r with angular speed ω and with linear speed v.", a: "a = v²/r = ω²r, directed towards the centre of the circle.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the angle of projection for maximum range on level ground? Write the maximum range in terms of u and g.", a: "θ = **45°**; the maximum range is R_max = u²/g.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Two forces of 7 N and 24 N act at right angles to each other on a body. Find the magnitude of the resultant and its direction with respect to the 7 N force.", a: "R = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = **25 N**.\ntanα = 24/7, so α = tan⁻¹(24/7) ≈ **73.7°** from the 7 N force.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A particle moves in a circle of radius 0.5 m with a period of 2 s. Find its speed and its centripetal acceleration.", a: "v = 2πr/T = 2π × 0.5/2 = π/2 ≈ **1.57 m/s**.\nω = 2π/T = π rad/s, so a = ω²r = π² × 0.5 ≈ **4.93 m/s²**, towards the centre.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Derive expressions for the time of flight, maximum height and horizontal range of a projectile fired with speed u at angle θ to the horizontal.", a: "Components: uₓ = u cosθ, u_y = u sinθ; the acceleration is −g vertically.\n1. Time of flight: y = 0 gives u sinθ·T − ½gT² = 0, so **T = 2u sinθ/g**.\n2. Maximum height: at the top v_y = 0, so v_y² = u_y² − 2gH gives **H = u² sin²θ/2g**.\n3. Range: R = uₓT = u cosθ × 2u sinθ/g = **u² sin2θ/g**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that two projectiles launched with the same speed at angles θ and (90° − θ) have the same range. Find the range for u = 30 m/s at 30° and at 60° (g = 10 m/s²).", a: "R = u² sin2θ/g. For the angle 90° − θ: sin(180° − 2θ) = sin2θ, so the range is the same.\nAt 30°: R = 900 × sin60°/10 = 90 × 0.866 ≈ **77.9 m**.\nAt 60°: R = 900 × sin120°/10 = 90 × 0.866 ≈ **77.9 m**. (The maximum range, at 45°, would be 90 m.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Given A = 2î + 3ĵ and B = 4î − ĵ, find (i) A + B, (ii) the magnitude of A − B, (iii) the scalar product A · B.", a: "(i) A + B = (2 + 4)î + (3 − 1)ĵ = **6î + 2ĵ**.\n(ii) A − B = −2î + 4ĵ, so |A − B| = √(4 + 16) = √20 = 2√5 ≈ **4.47**.\n(iii) A · B = (2)(4) + (3)(−1) = 8 − 3 = **5**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A swimmer who can swim at 5 m/s in still water wants to cross a 200 m wide river flowing at 3 m/s. Find (i) the time taken and the downstream drift if she heads perpendicular to the bank, (ii) the direction she must head and the time taken to reach the point exactly opposite her starting point.", a: "(i) Heading perpendicular: t = 200/5 = **40 s**; drift = 3 × 40 = **120 m** downstream.\n(ii) To go straight across, her upstream component must cancel the current: 5 sinθ = 3, sinθ = 3/5, so θ ≈ **37° upstream of the perpendicular**.\nSpeed across = 5 cosθ = √(25 − 9) = 4 m/s, so t = 200/4 = **50 s**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A stone is projected from level ground with a speed of 50 m/s at 53° above the horizontal (sin53° = 0.8, cos53° = 0.6, g = 10 m/s²). Find (i) the time of flight, (ii) the maximum height, (iii) the range, (iv) the magnitude and direction of the velocity at t = 2 s.", a: "uₓ = 30 m/s, u_y = 40 m/s.\n(i) T = 2u_y/g = 80/10 = **8 s**.\n(ii) H = u_y²/2g = 1600/20 = **80 m**.\n(iii) R = uₓT = 30 × 8 = **240 m**.\n(iv) At t = 2 s: vₓ = 30 m/s, v_y = 40 − 10 × 2 = 20 m/s. v = √(900 + 400) = √1300 ≈ **36.1 m/s**, at tan⁻¹(20/30) ≈ **33.7° above the horizontal**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A car moves at a constant speed of 20 m/s round a circular track of radius 100 m.\n(i) Find its centripetal acceleration.\n(ii) Find the time taken for one lap.\n(iii) Find the magnitude of the change in its velocity as it goes through a quarter of the circle.", a: "(i) a = v²/r = 400/100 = **4 m/s²**, directed towards the centre.\n(ii) T = 2πr/v = 2π × 100/20 = 10π ≈ **31.4 s**.\n(iii) After a quarter circle the velocity has turned by 90°, so the two velocities are perpendicular: |Δv| = √(20² + 20²) = 20√2 ≈ **28.3 m/s**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "laws-of-motion": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The inertia of a body is a measure of its:", options: ["velocity", "mass", "shape", "volume"], answer: 1, explain: "Inertia is the resistance to a change in the state of motion, and its measure is the mass of the body.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of impulse is:", options: ["N m", "N s⁻¹", "N s", "kg m s⁻²"], answer: 2, explain: "Impulse = force × time, so its unit is N s (the same as kg m s⁻¹, the unit of momentum).", level: 1 },
      { q: "For the same pair of surfaces, the limiting value of static friction is, in general:", options: ["smaller than kinetic friction", "equal to the weight of the body", "unrelated to kinetic friction", "greater than kinetic friction"], answer: 3, explain: "It takes a bigger force to start sliding than to keep it going, so μₛ is generally greater than μₖ.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The action and reaction forces of Newton's third law:", options: ["act on different bodies", "act on the same body", "are always unequal in magnitude", "do not act at the same time"], answer: 0, explain: "They are equal and opposite and act simultaneously, but on two different bodies, so they never cancel each other.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A passenger standing in a bus is thrown forward when the bus stops suddenly. This is due to:", options: ["inertia of rest", "inertia of direction", "inertia of motion", "the force of gravity"], answer: 2, explain: "The body of the passenger tends to continue in its state of motion while the bus slows down.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A 60 kg person stands on a weighing scale in a lift that accelerates upward at 2 m/s² (g = 10 m/s²). The scale reads:", options: ["600 N", "480 N", "1200 N", "720 N"], answer: 3, explain: "N − mg = ma, so N = m(g + a) = 60 × 12 = 720 N.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A cricketer catches a 0.16 kg ball moving at 25 m/s and brings it to rest in 0.02 s. The average force on his hands is:", options: ["200 N", "4 N", "80 N", "2000 N"], answer: 0, explain: "F = Δp/Δt = 0.16 × 25/0.02 = 4/0.02 = 200 N. Drawing the hands back increases Δt and reduces F.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): It is easier to pull a lawn roller than to push it.\nReason (R): Pushing at an angle increases the normal reaction, and so increases the friction.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "When pushing, the vertical component of the force adds to the weight (N = mg + F sinθ); when pulling it subtracts (N = mg − F sinθ). Hence less friction on pulling.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A body at rest has no force acting on it.\nReason (R): The net force on a body at rest is zero.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A book on a table has weight and normal reaction acting on it; they cancel. So A is false while R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A gun of mass 4 kg fires a bullet of mass 20 g with a speed of 300 m/s. The recoil speed of the gun is:", options: ["15 m/s", "6 m/s", "1.5 m/s", "0.15 m/s"], answer: 2, explain: "Momentum conservation: 4 V = 0.02 × 300 = 6, so V = 1.5 m/s.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A block of 3 kg on a smooth horizontal table is connected by a light string over a smooth pulley to a 2 kg block hanging freely (g = 10 m/s²). The tension in the string is:", options: ["20 N", "8 N", "12 N", "10 N"], answer: 2, explain: "a = m₂g/(m₁ + m₂) = 20/5 = 4 m/s². T = m₁a = 3 × 4 = 12 N (check: 20 − 2 × 4 = 12 N).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 2 kg block slides down a rough incline of 37° (sin37° = 0.6, cos37° = 0.8) with μₖ = 0.5 (g = 10 m/s²). Its acceleration is:", options: ["2 m/s²", "6 m/s²", "1 m/s²", "4 m/s²"], answer: 0, explain: "a = g(sinθ − μ cosθ) = 10(0.6 − 0.5 × 0.8) = 10 × 0.2 = 2 m/s². The mass cancels.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A car travels round a level circular curve of radius 20 m on which the coefficient of static friction is 0.5 (g = 10 m/s²). The maximum speed at which it can turn without skidding is:", options: ["10 km/h", "18 km/h", "72 km/h", "36 km/h"], answer: 3, explain: "v = √(μₛRg) = √(0.5 × 20 × 10) = 10 m/s = 36 km/h.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Masses of 3 kg and 5 kg hang from the two ends of a light string passing over a smooth, light fixed pulley (g = 10 m/s²). The force exerted by the string on the pulley's support is:", options: ["80 N", "75 N", "37.5 N", "50 N"], answer: 1, explain: "a = (5 − 3)g/8 = 2.5 m/s², T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂) = 2 × 15 × 10/8 = 37.5 N. The support carries 2T = 75 N, less than the total weight 80 N since the system accelerates.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Newton's second law of motion and give the relation between force, mass and acceleration.", a: "The rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction of the force: F = dp/dt. For constant mass this gives **F = ma**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define impulse of a force. State its SI unit.", a: "Impulse is the product of the force and the time for which it acts, J = FΔt, and equals the change in momentum of the body. SI unit: **N s** (equivalently kg m s⁻¹).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A 0.5 kg ball moving at 10 m/s strikes a wall normally and rebounds at 6 m/s. If the contact lasts 0.01 s, find the impulse and the average force on the ball.", a: "Taking the initial direction as positive: Δp = 0.5 × (−6) − 0.5 × 10 = −8 kg m/s.\nImpulse magnitude = **8 N s**, directed away from the wall.\nAverage force F = 8/0.01 = **800 N**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Arrange static, kinetic and rolling friction in order of magnitude for the same body and surface, and explain why wheels are used to move heavy loads.", a: "Static (limiting) friction > kinetic (sliding) friction > rolling friction.\nIn rolling, the contact point is momentarily at rest and the surfaces do not rub against each other, so the resistance is much smaller than for sliding; hence wheels make it easier to move heavy loads.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Using Newton's second and third laws, show that the total momentum of an isolated two-body system is conserved.", a: "1. Let bodies A and B exert forces F_AB and F_BA on each other during a collision; there is no external force.\n2. By the second law, F_AB = dp_B/dt and F_BA = dp_A/dt.\n3. By the third law, F_AB = −F_BA, so dp_A/dt + dp_B/dt = 0, i.e. d(p_A + p_B)/dt = 0.\nHence **p_A + p_B = constant**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A 10 kg block lies on a rough horizontal floor (μₖ = 0.2, g = 10 m/s²). It is pulled by a 50 N force directed 37° above the horizontal (sin37° = 0.6, cos37° = 0.8). Find its acceleration.", a: "Vertical: N + 50 sin37° = mg, so N = 100 − 30 = 70 N.\nFriction f = μₖN = 0.2 × 70 = 14 N.\nHorizontal: 50 cos37° − f = ma → 40 − 14 = 10a.\n**a = 2.6 m/s²**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A car of mass 1000 kg moves on a level circular road of radius 50 m with μₛ = 0.4 (g = 10 m/s²). Find the maximum safe speed. Will the car skid at 72 km/h? Does the answer depend on the mass of the car?", a: "Friction provides the centripetal force: μₛmg = mv²/R, so v_max = √(μₛRg) = √(0.4 × 50 × 10) = √200 ≈ **14.1 m/s** (about 51 km/h).\n72 km/h = 20 m/s > 14.1 m/s, so the car **will skid**.\nThe mass cancels in the expression, so the safe speed is **independent of the mass**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Two blocks of masses 4 kg and 6 kg are in contact on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force of 30 N pushes the 4 kg block towards the 6 kg block. Find (i) the acceleration of the system, (ii) the contact force between the blocks, (iii) the contact force if the same 30 N is applied to the 6 kg block instead, pushing it towards the 4 kg block.", a: "(i) a = F/(m₁ + m₂) = 30/10 = **3 m/s²**.\n(ii) The 6 kg block is accelerated only by the contact force: N = 6 × 3 = **18 N**. (Check on the 4 kg block: 30 − 18 = 12 = 4 × 3 ✓.)\n(iii) Now the 4 kg block is accelerated only by the contact force: N = 4 × 3 = **12 N**.\nThe acceleration is still 3 m/s²; the contact force is smaller because the block being pushed has less mass.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the optimum speed on a banked road without friction. Find the angle of banking for a curve of radius 100 m designed for 20 m/s (g = 10 m/s²).", a: "1. Forces on the vehicle: weight mg downward and normal reaction N perpendicular to the road, inclined at θ to the vertical.\n2. Vertical: N cosθ = mg.\n3. Horizontal (towards the centre): N sinθ = mv²/R.\n4. Dividing: tanθ = v²/Rg, so **v₀ = √(Rg tanθ)**; it is independent of mass.\n5. tanθ = 400/(100 × 10) = 0.4, so θ = tan⁻¹(0.4) ≈ **21.8°**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A truck carries a crate of mass 100 kg on its flat bed. The coefficient of static friction between the crate and the bed is 0.5 (g = 10 m/s²).\n(i) Find the maximum friction force that can act on the crate.\n(ii) Find the maximum acceleration of the truck for which the crate does not slip.\n(iii) The truck brakes with a deceleration of 4 m/s². Does the crate slide? Give a reason.", a: "(i) f_max = μₛmg = 0.5 × 100 × 10 = **500 N**.\n(ii) Friction alone accelerates the crate: a_max = f_max/m = 500/100 = **5 m/s²**.\n(iii) The friction force needed to decelerate the crate at 4 m/s² is 100 × 4 = 400 N, which is less than 500 N, so static friction can supply it: the crate **does not slide**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "work-energy-and-power": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of power is:", options: ["joule", "watt", "newton", "pascal"], answer: 1, explain: "Power is the rate of doing work; 1 watt = 1 joule per second.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The work done by a force acting perpendicular to the displacement of its point of application is:", options: ["zero", "F d", "negative", "half of F d"], answer: 0, explain: "W = F d cosθ, and cos90° = 0. Gravity does no work on a body moving horizontally, for example.", level: 1 },
      { q: "One kilowatt-hour is equal to:", options: ["3.6 × 10³ J", "3.6 × 10⁶ J", "3.6 × 10⁵ J", "10³ J"], answer: 1, explain: "1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a perfectly elastic collision, which of the following quantities is conserved?", options: ["only momentum", "only kinetic energy", "total energy but not momentum", "both momentum and kinetic energy"], answer: 3, explain: "In an elastic collision the total momentum and the total kinetic energy are both conserved.", level: 1 },
      { q: "According to the work–energy theorem, the work done by the net force on a body equals the change in its:", options: ["potential energy", "momentum", "kinetic energy", "total mechanical energy"], answer: 2, explain: "W_net = K_f − K_i.", level: 1 },

      { q: "If the momentum of a body increases by 50%, its kinetic energy increases by:", options: ["50%", "100%", "125%", "225%"], answer: 2, explain: "K = p²/2m. With p → 1.5p, K → 2.25K, an increase of 125%.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A motor pumps 1200 kg of water every minute from a well 20 m deep and delivers it at ground level with negligible speed (g = 10 m/s²). The power it must supply is:", options: ["240 kW", "4 kW", "400 W", "40 kW"], answer: 1, explain: "Work per minute = mgh = 1200 × 10 × 20 = 2.4 × 10⁵ J. Power = 2.4 × 10⁵/60 = 4000 W = 4 kW.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The work done by a conservative force over a closed path is zero.\nReason (R): The work done by a conservative force depends only on the end points of the path.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "For a closed path the end points coincide, so a work that depends only on the end points must be zero.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In a perfectly inelastic collision, some kinetic energy is lost.\nReason (R): In inelastic collisions, the total momentum of the system is not conserved.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Kinetic energy is converted to heat, sound and deformation, so A is true. Momentum is conserved in every collision when no external force acts, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A toy gun has a spring of constant 200 N/m compressed by 0.1 m. On release it fires a 20 g ball along a smooth horizontal surface. The speed of the ball is:", options: ["5 m/s", "20 m/s", "100 m/s", "10 m/s"], answer: 3, explain: "½kx² = ½mv²: ½ × 200 × 0.01 = 1 J = ½ × 0.02 × v², so v² = 100 and v = 10 m/s.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A force F = (3x² + 2x) N acts on a particle along the x-axis. The work done in moving it from x = 0 to x = 2 m is:", options: ["12 J", "16 J", "8 J", "20 J"], answer: 0, explain: "W = ∫(3x² + 2x)dx = [x³ + x²] from 0 to 2 = 8 + 4 = 12 J.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 10 g bullet moving at 400 m/s embeds itself in a wooden block and stops after penetrating 0.2 m. The average resistive force is:", options: ["800 N", "4000 N", "8000 N", "2000 N"], answer: 1, explain: "Work–energy: F × d = ½mv² = ½ × 0.01 × 400² = 800 J. So F = 800/0.2 = 4000 N.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 0.5 kg pendulum bob on a 1 m string is released from the horizontal position (g = 10 m/s²). The tension in the string at the lowest point is:", options: ["5 N", "10 N", "15 N", "20 N"], answer: 2, explain: "Energy: v² = 2gl = 20 m²/s². At the bottom T − mg = mv²/l, so T = 5 + 0.5 × 20/1 = 15 N (= 3mg).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A body of mass 2 kg moving at 6 m/s collides head-on and sticks to a 4 kg body at rest. The fraction of the initial kinetic energy lost is:", options: ["1/3", "1/2", "3/4", "2/3"], answer: 3, explain: "Common speed = 12/6 = 2 m/s. Initial K = ½ × 2 × 36 = 36 J; final K = ½ × 6 × 4 = 12 J. Loss = 24 J, which is 2/3 of the original.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the work–energy theorem.", a: "The work done by the net force on a body is equal to the change in its kinetic energy: W_net = ½mv² − ½mu².", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define power. Write its SI unit and its dimensional formula.", a: "Power is the rate at which work is done, P = W/t. SI unit: watt (J s⁻¹). Dimensions: **[M L² T⁻³]**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A 5 kg body at rest is acted on by a constant force of 20 N through a distance of 10 m on a smooth horizontal surface. Find the work done and the final speed.", a: "W = F s = 20 × 10 = **200 J**.\nBy the work–energy theorem ½ × 5 × v² = 200, so v² = 80 and v = √80 ≈ **8.9 m/s**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Two bodies of masses 1 kg and 4 kg have equal kinetic energies. Find the ratio of their momenta.", a: "p = √(2mK). For equal K, p ∝ √m.\np₁ : p₂ = √1 : √4 = **1 : 2**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Derive the work–energy theorem for a body moving under a constant force.", a: "1. For constant acceleration a over displacement s: v² − u² = 2as.\n2. Multiply by m/2: ½mv² − ½mu² = (ma)s.\n3. Since F = ma, the right side is F s = W.\nHence **W = ½mv² − ½mu² = ΔK**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the potential energy stored in a spring stretched by x is ½kx². Find the energy stored in a spring of constant 500 N/m stretched by 4 cm.", a: "The restoring force is F = kx; the external force needed to stretch it slowly equals kx, which varies with x.\nWork done = ∫₀ˣ kx dx = ½kx² (the area of the triangle under the F–x graph), stored as potential energy.\nNumerical: U = ½ × 500 × (0.04)² = ½ × 500 × 0.0016 = **0.4 J**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A 2 kg block slides from rest down an incline whose top is 5 m above its bottom (g = 10 m/s²). Find its speed at the bottom if the incline is smooth. If it actually reaches the bottom at 8 m/s, find the work done against friction.", a: "Smooth: mgh = ½mv² → v = √(2gh) = √100 = **10 m/s**.\nRough: loss of PE = mgh = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J; gain of KE = ½ × 2 × 8² = 64 J.\nWork done against friction = 100 − 64 = **36 J**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A body of mass m₁ moving with speed u₁ collides head-on and elastically with a body of mass m₂ at rest. Derive the velocities after collision and discuss the cases (i) m₁ = m₂, (ii) m₁ ≫ m₂, (iii) m₁ ≪ m₂.", a: "Momentum: m₁u₁ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ … (1). Kinetic energy: m₁u₁² = m₁v₁² + m₂v₂² … (2).\nFrom (1) and (2): m₁(u₁ − v₁) = m₂v₂ and m₁(u₁² − v₁²) = m₂v₂², dividing gives u₁ + v₁ = v₂.\nSubstituting in (1): **v₁ = (m₁ − m₂)u₁/(m₁ + m₂)** and **v₂ = 2m₁u₁/(m₁ + m₂)**.\n(i) m₁ = m₂: v₁ = 0, v₂ = u₁; the velocities are exchanged.\n(ii) m₁ ≫ m₂: v₁ ≈ u₁, v₂ ≈ 2u₁; the heavy body is hardly affected.\n(iii) m₁ ≪ m₂: v₁ ≈ −u₁, v₂ ≈ 0; the light body bounces back with almost the same speed, as a ball off a wall.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A particle of mass 0.5 kg, initially at rest at x = 0, is acted on by a force F = 4x N (x in metres) along the x-axis. Find (i) the work done as it moves to x = 5 m, (ii) its speed at x = 5 m, (iii) verify the work done from the area of the F–x graph.", a: "(i) W = ∫₀⁵ 4x dx = [2x²]₀⁵ = 2 × 25 = **50 J**.\n(ii) By the work–energy theorem ½mv² = 50, so v² = 2 × 50/0.5 = 200 and v = **√200 ≈ 14.1 m/s**.\n(iii) The F–x graph is a straight line from 0 to F = 20 N at x = 5 m. Area of the triangle = ½ × 5 × 20 = **50 J**, which agrees with (i).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A car of mass 1200 kg has an engine that delivers a constant power of 36 kW. The total resistive force on the car is a constant 600 N.\n(i) Find the maximum speed of the car on a level road.\n(ii) Find the acceleration of the car when its speed is 20 m/s.\n(iii) Find the energy supplied by the engine in 30 minutes of running at full power, in kWh and in joule.", a: "(i) At maximum speed the driving force equals the resistance: v_max = P/f = 36000/600 = **60 m/s**.\n(ii) At 20 m/s the driving force is P/v = 36000/20 = 1800 N. Net force = 1800 − 600 = 1200 N, so a = 1200/1200 = **1 m/s²**.\n(iii) E = P t = 36 kW × 0.5 h = **18 kWh** = 18 × 3.6 × 10⁶ = **6.48 × 10⁷ J**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "system-of-particles-and-rotational-motion": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of moment of inertia is:", options: ["kg m", "kg m²", "kg m² s⁻¹", "N m"], answer: 1, explain: "I = Σmr², so its unit is kg m². (kg m² s⁻¹ is the unit of angular momentum and N m is that of torque.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "The torque of a force about a point is zero if the line of action of the force:", options: ["is perpendicular to the position vector", "is very small in magnitude", "passes through the point", "is at the largest distance from the point"], answer: 2, explain: "τ = rF sinθ; if the line of action passes through the point, the perpendicular distance (or sinθ) is zero.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A skater spinning on ice pulls her arms in. Her angular velocity:", options: ["increases", "decreases", "remains the same", "becomes zero"], answer: 0, explain: "No external torque acts, so Iω is constant. Pulling the arms in reduces I, so ω increases.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The moment of inertia of a thin ring of mass M and radius R about the axis through its centre perpendicular to its plane is:", options: ["½MR²", "⅖MR²", "ML²/12", "MR²"], answer: 3, explain: "All the mass is at distance R from the axis, so I = MR².", level: 1 },
      { q: "In rotational motion, the quantity that plays the role of mass in translational motion is:", options: ["torque", "angular momentum", "moment of inertia", "angular velocity"], answer: 2, explain: "τ = Iα is the analogue of F = ma, so moment of inertia corresponds to mass.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A flywheel of moment of inertia 0.4 kg m² is initially at rest and a constant torque of 2 N m acts on it for 10 s. Its rotational kinetic energy at the end is:", options: ["250 J", "1000 J", "5000 J", "500 J"], answer: 3, explain: "α = τ/I = 5 rad/s², ω = αt = 50 rad/s. K = ½Iω² = ½ × 0.4 × 2500 = 500 J.", level: 2 },
      { q: "On a seesaw, a 20 kg child sits 1.5 m from the pivot. A 30 kg adult must sit at what distance on the other side to balance it?", options: ["1 m", "2.25 m", "0.5 m", "1.5 m"], answer: 0, explain: "Principle of moments: 20 × 1.5 = 30 × d, so d = 1 m.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A body can be in translational equilibrium without being in rotational equilibrium.\nReason (R): A couple has zero net force but a non-zero net torque.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Under a couple the net force is zero (translational equilibrium) but the torque is not (it rotates). R correctly explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A person on a rotating stool stretches her arms outward and her angular speed decreases.\nReason (R): When she stretches her arms, her moment of inertia decreases.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Iω is conserved. Stretching the arms increases I, so ω decreases: A is true and R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A mechanic applies a force of 40 N at the end of a 0.25 m spanner, at 30° to the length of the spanner. The torque about the nut is:", options: ["10 N m", "5 N m", "8.7 N m", "20 N m"], answer: 1, explain: "τ = rF sinθ = 0.25 × 40 × sin30° = 5 N m.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A turntable of moment of inertia 0.5 kg m² rotates at 6 rad/s about its axis. A 1 kg lump of clay is dropped gently on its rim, 0.5 m from the axis, and sticks. The new angular speed is:", options: ["4 rad/s", "3 rad/s", "6 rad/s", "2 rad/s"], answer: 0, explain: "L = 0.5 × 6 = 3 kg m²/s. New I = 0.5 + 1 × 0.5² = 0.75 kg m². ω′ = 3/0.75 = 4 rad/s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Four particles of mass 1 kg each are at the corners of a square of side 2 m. The moment of inertia of the system about an axis through the centre of the square and perpendicular to its plane is:", options: ["2 kg m²", "4 kg m²", "16 kg m²", "8 kg m²"], answer: 3, explain: "Each particle is at distance √(2² + 2²)/2 = √2 m from the centre. I = 4 × 1 × (√2)² = 8 kg m².", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 2 kg mass hangs from a light string wound round a pulley of moment of inertia 0.5 kg m² and radius 0.5 m, and is released (g = 10 m/s²). The tension in the string is:", options: ["20 N", "10 N", "15 N", "5 N"], answer: 1, explain: "mg − T = ma and TR = Iα = Ia/R, so T = Ia/R² = 2a. Then 20 − T = 2a gives a = 5 m/s² and T = 10 N.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A shell fired from level ground with horizontal speed 30 m/s and vertical speed 40 m/s explodes at the top of its path into two equal fragments. One fragment falls vertically downward with no horizontal velocity. Taking g = 10 m/s², the other fragment lands at what horizontal distance from the firing point?", options: ["240 m", "300 m", "360 m", "480 m"], answer: 2, explain: "Without the explosion the range would be 30 × 8 = 240 m, with the top at x = 120 m. Horizontal momentum: M × 30 = (M/2) × v, so v = 60 m/s. Fall time from 80 m is 4 s, so it travels 240 m more: 120 + 240 = 360 m. (The CM lands at (120 + 360)/2 = 240 m.)", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define moment of inertia and state its SI unit.", a: "Moment of inertia of a body about an axis is I = Σmᵢrᵢ², the sum of the products of the mass of each particle and the square of its perpendicular distance from the axis; it is the rotational analogue of mass. SI unit: **kg m²**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A shell explodes in mid-air. Why does the centre of mass of the fragments continue along the original parabolic path?", a: "The forces of the explosion are internal and do not change the motion of the centre of mass. Only the external force (gravity) acts, so the centre of mass follows the same parabola (until a fragment hits the ground).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the centre of mass of three particles: 1 kg at (0, 0), 2 kg at (3 m, 0) and 3 kg at (0, 4 m).", a: "M = 6 kg.\nX = (1 × 0 + 2 × 3 + 3 × 0)/6 = 6/6 = 1 m.\nY = (1 × 0 + 2 × 0 + 3 × 4)/6 = 12/6 = 2 m.\nCentre of mass at **(1 m, 2 m)**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A wheel of moment of inertia 4 kg m² rotating at 10 rad/s is brought to rest by a constant torque in 5 s. Find the angular retardation and the torque.", a: "α = (0 − 10)/5 = −2, so the retardation is **2 rad/s²**.\nτ = Iα = 4 × 2 = **8 N m** (opposing the rotation).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that the torque on a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis is τ = Iα.", a: "1. Consider a particle of mass mᵢ at distance rᵢ from the axis. Its tangential acceleration is rᵢα, so the tangential force on it is mᵢrᵢα.\n2. Its torque about the axis is rᵢ × mᵢrᵢα = mᵢrᵢ²α.\n3. Summing over all particles, the internal torques cancel in pairs, so τ(ext) = (Σmᵢrᵢ²)α = **Iα**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A skater with moment of inertia 4 kg m² spins at 3 rad/s with arms outstretched. She pulls her arms in so that I becomes 1.5 kg m². Find her new angular speed and compare the kinetic energies. Account for the difference.", a: "No external torque, so I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂: ω₂ = 4 × 3/1.5 = **8 rad/s**.\nK₁ = ½ × 4 × 3² = 18 J; K₂ = ½ × 1.5 × 8² = 48 J.\nThe kinetic energy increases by **30 J**; this comes from the work done by the skater's muscles in pulling her arms inward while they are moving in circles.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A uniform thin rod of mass 3 kg and length 2 m rotates about an axis through its centre perpendicular to its length. Find its moment of inertia and radius of gyration.", a: "I = ML²/12 = 3 × 2²/12 = **1 kg m²**.\nI = Mk² gives k = √(I/M) = √(1/3) ≈ **0.58 m**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A uniform plank of mass 20 kg and length 4 m rests on supports at its two ends A and B. A boy of mass 40 kg stands 1 m from end A (g = 10 m/s²). State the conditions of mechanical equilibrium and find the reactions at A and B.", a: "Conditions: net force = 0 and net torque = 0.\nWeights: plank 200 N at the centre (2 m from A); boy 400 N at 1 m from A.\nTorque about B (4 m from A): R_A × 4 = 200 × 2 + 400 × 3 = 1600, so **R_A = 400 N**.\nForce balance: R_A + R_B = 600, so **R_B = 200 N**.\nCheck about A: R_B × 4 = 200 × 2 + 400 × 1 = 800 → R_B = 200 N ✓.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A uniform disc of mass 2 kg and radius 0.5 m is free to rotate about its axis. A tangential force of 5 N is applied at the rim for 4 s, starting from rest. Find the torque, the angular acceleration, the final angular velocity, the angular momentum and the rotational kinetic energy.", a: "I = ½MR² = ½ × 2 × 0.25 = 0.25 kg m².\nTorque τ = FR = 5 × 0.5 = **2.5 N m**.\nα = τ/I = 2.5/0.25 = **10 rad/s²**.\nω = αt = 10 × 4 = **40 rad/s**.\nL = Iω = 0.25 × 40 = **10 kg m²/s**.\nK = ½Iω² = ½ × 0.25 × 1600 = **200 J**.\n(Check: θ = ½αt² = 80 rad, work = τθ = 2.5 × 80 = 200 J ✓.)", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A playground merry-go-round (treat it as a disc) has a moment of inertia of 480 kg m² and rotates freely at 2 rad/s. A child of mass 40 kg runs towards it and jumps on at its edge, 2 m from the axis.\n(i) Find the angular momentum of the system before the jump (take the child's angular momentum about the axis as zero).\n(ii) Find the angular velocity after the child has jumped on.\n(iii) Find the loss of kinetic energy.", a: "(i) L = Iω = 480 × 2 = **960 kg m²/s**.\n(ii) New I = 480 + 40 × 2² = 640 kg m². No external torque, so ω′ = 960/640 = **1.5 rad/s**.\n(iii) K before = ½ × 480 × 2² = 960 J; K after = ½ × 640 × 1.5² = 720 J. Loss = **240 J** (converted to heat and other forms in the sticking process).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "gravitation": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Kepler's law of areas (equal areas in equal times) is a consequence of the conservation of:", options: ["linear momentum", "angular momentum", "energy", "mass"], answer: 1, explain: "Gravity is a central force, so the torque about the Sun is zero and the planet's angular momentum is conserved. This gives a constant areal velocity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The acceleration due to gravity at the centre of the earth (taken as a uniform sphere) is:", options: ["9.8 m/s²", "infinite", "4.9 m/s²", "zero"], answer: 3, explain: "g(d) = g(1 − d/R) becomes zero when d = R.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The escape speed from the surface of the earth is about:", options: ["11.2 km/s", "7.9 km/s", "9.8 km/s", "15.4 km/s"], answer: 0, explain: "vₑ = √(2gR) = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) ≈ 11.2 km/s.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of the universal gravitational constant G is:", options: ["N m kg⁻¹", "N kg² m⁻²", "N m² kg⁻²", "N m⁻² kg²"], answer: 2, explain: "G = Fr²/(m₁m₂), so its unit is N m² kg⁻².", level: 1 },
      { q: "The total energy of a satellite orbiting the earth is:", options: ["positive", "negative", "zero", "dependent on its mass only"], answer: 1, explain: "E = −GMm/2r < 0, which shows the satellite is bound to the earth.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The International Space Station orbits 400 km above the earth's surface. Taking g = 9.8 m/s² and the radius of the earth as 6400 km, its orbital speed is nearly:", options: ["7.9 km/s", "6.9 km/s", "8.4 km/s", "7.7 km/s"], answer: 3, explain: "v = √(GM/(R + h)) = R√(g/(R + h)) = 6.4 × 10⁶ × √(9.8/6.8 × 10⁶) ≈ 7.7 × 10³ m/s. It is slightly less than the 7.9 km/s of a surface-skimming orbit.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A television channel is relayed by a satellite that appears to stay fixed above one point in the sky. For this, the satellite must:", options: ["orbit above the poles with a period of 12 h", "orbit in the equatorial plane with a period of 12 h", "orbit in the equatorial plane with a period of 24 h, in the same sense as the earth's rotation", "orbit in any plane with a period of 24 h"], answer: 2, explain: "A geostationary satellite must be in the equatorial plane, have the earth's rotation period (24 h) and move west to east like the earth.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The escape speed from the earth does not depend on the mass of the body projected.\nReason (R): The escape speed is √(2GM/R), which involves only the mass and radius of the earth.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Equating ½mv² to GMm/R, m cancels, leaving vₑ = √(2GM/R).", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): An astronaut inside an orbiting satellite feels weightless.\nReason (R): The gravitational pull of the earth on the astronaut is zero at the height of the orbit.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "The astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall with the same acceleration, so there is no contact force: A is true. Gravity at that height is still large (about 90% of g at 400 km), so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A planet moves round the Sun in a circular orbit whose radius is 4 times that of the earth's orbit. Its period in earth years is:", options: ["4", "8", "16", "64"], answer: 1, explain: "T² ∝ r³, so T = 1 × 4^(3/2) = 8 years.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The value of g at a height h above the earth's surface is the same as its value at a depth d below the surface. If both h and d are very small compared with the earth's radius, then:", options: ["d = h", "d = 2h", "d = h/2", "d = 4h"], answer: 1, explain: "g(1 − 2h/R) = g(1 − d/R) gives d = 2h.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A planet has 8 times the mass of the earth and twice its radius. The escape speed from its surface is:", options: ["11.2 km/s", "15.8 km/s", "22.4 km/s", "44.8 km/s"], answer: 2, explain: "vₑ ∝ √(M/R) = √(8/2) = 2, so vₑ = 2 × 11.2 = 22.4 km/s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 500 kg satellite moves in a circular orbit at a height equal to the radius of the earth (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m). The energy needed to take it out of the earth's gravitational field is:", options: ["1.57 × 10¹⁰ J", "3.92 × 10⁹ J", "3.14 × 10¹⁰ J", "7.84 × 10⁹ J"], answer: 3, explain: "Orbit radius r = 2R. Binding energy = GMm/2r = gR²m/(4R) = gRm/4 = 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶ × 500/4 = 7.84 × 10⁹ J.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A satellite in a low circular orbit slowly loses mechanical energy because of air drag. Its orbital radius and orbital speed will, respectively:", options: ["decrease and increase", "increase and increase", "decrease and decrease", "increase and decrease"], answer: 0, explain: "E = −GMm/2r becomes more negative, so r decreases. Since v = √(GM/r), the speed increases (the drag does negative work but the fall into the lower orbit more than compensates).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Kepler's law of periods.", a: "The square of the time period of revolution of a planet round the Sun is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of its elliptical orbit: T² ∝ a³.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define escape speed. State its value for the earth.", a: "Escape speed is the minimum speed with which a body must be projected from the surface of a planet so that it never returns to it. For the earth, vₑ = √(2gR) ≈ **11.2 km/s**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Two spheres of masses 10 kg and 20 kg have their centres 0.5 m apart. Find the gravitational force between them (G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²).", a: "F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 10 × 20/(0.5)²\n= 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 200/0.25 = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 800\n≈ **5.34 × 10⁻⁸ N**, attractive.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the value of g at a height of 3200 km above the earth's surface (R = 6400 km, g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface).", a: "h = R/2, so r = 1.5R.\ng′ = gR²/(R + h)² = 9.8/(1.5)² = 9.8/2.25 ≈ **4.36 m/s²**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that the acceleration due to gravity at a depth d below the earth's surface is g′ = g(1 − d/R).", a: "1. At depth d, only the sphere of radius (R − d) attracts the body (the outer shell exerts no net force).\n2. Its mass is M′ = M(R − d)³/R³ for uniform density.\n3. g′ = GM′/(R − d)² = GM(R − d)/R³ = (GM/R²)(1 − d/R).\nHence **g′ = g(1 − d/R)**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the escape speed from the earth's surface and calculate its value (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m).", a: "1. Gravitational potential energy at the surface = −GMm/R; at infinity it is zero.\n2. The kinetic energy needed is ½mvₑ² = GMm/R, so vₑ = √(2GM/R) = √(2gR) (since GM = gR²).\n3. vₑ = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) = √(1.2544 × 10⁸) = 1.12 × 10⁴ m/s = **11.2 km/s**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A satellite orbits the earth at a height equal to the earth's radius (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m). Find its orbital speed and period.", a: "Orbit radius r = 2R = 1.28 × 10⁷ m.\nv = √(GM/r) = √(gR²/2R) = √(gR/2) = √(9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶/2) = √(3.136 × 10⁷) = **5600 m/s = 5.6 km/s**.\nT = 2πr/v = 2π × 1.28 × 10⁷/5600 ≈ 1.44 × 10⁴ s ≈ **4.0 h**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State Kepler's three laws of planetary motion. Show that for a planet in a circular orbit of radius r about the Sun T² ∝ r³, and find the period of a planet whose orbital radius is 4 times that of the earth.", a: "Laws: (1) planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one focus; (2) the line joining a planet to the Sun sweeps equal areas in equal times; (3) T² ∝ a³.\nDerivation: gravity supplies the centripetal force: GMm/r² = mω²r = m(4π²/T²)r.\nSo T² = (4π²/GM) r³, i.e. **T² ∝ r³**.\nFor r = 4r_E: T = T_E × 4^(3/2) = 1 × 8 = **8 years**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A satellite of mass 200 kg orbits the earth at a distance of 2R from the centre (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m). Find its kinetic energy, potential energy, total energy and binding energy.", a: "GM = gR². At r = 2R: GMm/r = gR²m/2R = gRm/2 = 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶ × 200/2 = 6.272 × 10⁹ J.\nPotential energy = −GMm/r = **−6.27 × 10⁹ J**.\nKinetic energy = GMm/2r = **+3.14 × 10⁹ J**.\nTotal energy = KE + PE = **−3.14 × 10⁹ J** (negative: the satellite is bound).\nBinding energy = −E = **3.14 × 10⁹ J**, the energy needed to free it from the earth's gravity.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Scientists plan a lander mission to Mars. Take the mass of Mars as 6.4 × 10²³ kg, its radius as 3.4 × 10⁶ m and G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².\n(i) Find the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of Mars.\n(ii) Find the escape speed from Mars.\n(iii) Find the weight on Mars of an astronaut of mass 60 kg.", a: "(i) g_M = GM/R² = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 6.4 × 10²³/(3.4 × 10⁶)² = 4.269 × 10¹³/1.156 × 10¹³ ≈ **3.7 m/s²**.\n(ii) vₑ = √(2g_M R) = √(2 × 3.69 × 3.4 × 10⁶) = √(2.51 × 10⁷) ≈ 5.0 × 10³ m/s = **5.0 km/s**.\n(iii) W = mg_M = 60 × 3.69 ≈ **222 N**, compared with about 588 N on the earth (mass stays 60 kg).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
